<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">United States Marine Corps</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as US Marine Corps</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Marine Corps is the maritime land force of the United States, founded in 1775 and serving under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Navy. In the late 19th and early 20th centuries Marines landed repeatedly in the Pacific and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, supporting the overthrow of the Hawaiian monarchy in 1893 and occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>. They later fought major amphibious campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps.jpg" alt="United States Marine Corps" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_United_States_Marine_Corps.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Corps traces its roots to the Continental Marines, formed by Captain Samuel Nicholas under a resolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress">Second Continental Congress</a> on 10 November 1775, which is marked as its birthday. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a> nominated Nicholas, who was commissioned a captain on 28 November 1775 and by December had raised one battalion of 300 men by recruitment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>.</p><p>The Marines were founded to serve as infantry aboard naval vessels. They guarded the ship and its crew in boarding actions and defended the officers against mutiny. The first American amphibious assault came on 3 March 1776, when Marines took Fort Montagu and Fort Nassau, a British ammunition depot and naval port on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-providence">New Providence</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>. The Corps has legally been part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Navy since 30 June 1834.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the War of 1812 Marine detachments took part in frigate duels, and they held the centre of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a>'s line at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> in 1815. At the Battle of Bladensburg on 24 August 1814, 120 marines under Captain Samuel Miller were among the only effective American resistance. In the Civil War about a third of the officers left to form the Confederate States Marine Corps. The Marines mainly performed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> duty and took part in the capture of New Orleans in 1862.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Corps grew from 511 officers and 13,214 enlisted personnel to 2,400 officers and 70,000 enlisted by 11 November 1918. Opha May Johnson became the first woman to enlist in 1918. Two manuals of the 1930s, the Small Wars Manual and the Tentative Landing Operations Manual, set doctrine for counterinsurgency and for the amphibious operations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a> the Marines fought at Guadalcanal, Tarawa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saipan">Saipan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peleliu">Peleliu</a>, Iwo Jima and Okinawa, and some 600,000 Americans served in the Corps in that war. In the Korean War Marines spearheaded the landing at Inchon, and the 1st Marine Division fought its way out from the Chosin Reservoir. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> 13,091 Marines were killed in action.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Marines are organised around the Marine Air-Ground Task Force, which joins ground, aviation and logistics elements under a common command. In the aftermath of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, Army leaders including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-c-marshall">George C. Marshall</a> sought to dissolve the Corps and fold its capabilities into the other services.</p><p>The Corps excluded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> from 1798 to 1942, and a presidential order forced it to begin recruiting them in 1942. Integration advanced from segregated battalions in 1942 to unified training in 1949 and full integration in 1960. Its traditions include nicknames such as Leatherneck, from a leather collar once part of the uniform, and the phrase Once a Marine, always a Marine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United States Marine Corps” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…wars between party chiefs continued into the twentieth century, often with foreign backing: in 1911 the former president Manuel Bonilla invaded with mercenaries financed by Samuel Zemurray and regained power, and <b>United States Marines</b> landed several times between 1903 and 1925, notably during the civil war of 1924.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>United States Marines</b> landed in 1912 to support a conservative government against a liberal rising, in which the nationalist general Benjamín Zeledón was killed, and a legation guard remained until 1925.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Second World War up to four hundred Navajo code talkers carried messages for the <b>US Marine Corps</b> in a code built on their own language, in which the word for “turtle” stood for a tank; the Japanese never deciphered it.</blockquote><a href="/culture/navajo">Navajo · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>An Act of Congress created the <b>US Marine Corps</b> as a permanent service, reviving the Continental Marines of the Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-11">11 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The date is celebrated as the birthday of the <b>United States Marine Corps</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-10">10 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After years of civil war and default on foreign debt, President Woodrow Wilson sent <b>Marines</b> to Santo Domingo in May 1916.</blockquote><a href="/state/military-government-of-santo-domingo">Military Government of Santo Domingo</a></li></ul></section>
